        "title": "5. The Inter-War Economy & The Great Depression (1929)",
        "points": [
          "**First World War (1914–1918):** First modern industrial war using machine guns, tanks, aircraft, and chemical weapons on a massive scale. Led to millions of deaths and injuries among working-age men. Economic transformation: Britain borrowed heavily from US banks; the **United States** transformed from an international debtor into an international creditor.",
          "**Mass Production & Henry Ford:** Pioneered in the 1920s by **Henry Ford** at his car plant in Detroit using the **assembly line** technique. Workers repeated a single mechanical task continuously. Result: Model T Ford car produced at record speed and lower cost. Higher wages ($5 a day) allowed workers to buy the very cars they made, creating a consumer boom (cars, refrigerators, washing machines, radios) financed by hire-purchase credit.",
          "**The Great Depression (1929–mid 1930s):** Causes: (1) **Agricultural overproduction:** Falling crop prices led farmers to expand production to maintain income, causing an even greater glut in the market, (2) **Withdrawal of US loans:** European countries depended on US loans; when US lenders panicked, credit collapsed.",
          "**Impact on the US:** The Wall Street stock market crashed in October 1929. Banks collapsed as borrowers defaulted on loans; over **4,000 banks failed** and 110,000 companies collapsed between 1929 and 1932. Widespread unemployment and homelessness.",
          "**Impact on India:** As global trade halved, India's exports and imports fell by 50% between 1928 and 1934. Wheat prices fell by 50%. Peasants and farmers suffered more than urban dwellers: while agricultural prices crashed, colonial revenue demands remained unchanged. Peasants fell deep into debt, mortgaged lands, and sold family jewellery and heirloom gold (**India became a net exporter of gold**, sustaining Britain's economy)."
        ]
