        "title": "6. Post-War Settlement, Bretton Woods & Decolonisation",
        "points": [
          "**Bretton Woods Conference (July 1944):** Held at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire (USA) to ensure economic stability and full employment in the post-war industrial world.",
          "**Bretton Woods Twins:** Established the **International Monetary Fund (IMF)** to deal with external surpluses and deficits of member nations, and the **International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (World Bank)** to finance post-war reconstruction.",
          "The system was based on **fixed exchange rates**: national currencies were pegged to the US Dollar, and the US Dollar was anchored to gold at a fixed price of **$35 per ounce**. Western powers and the US held veto power over key IMF/World Bank decisions.",
          "**Decolonisation and the G-77:** Newly independent nations did not benefit equally from Western-centric Bretton Woods institutions. Most developing countries organized themselves as the **Group of 77 (G-77)** to demand a **New International Economic Order (NIEO)**.",
          "**NIEO Demands:** (1) Real control over their natural resources, (2) Fairer prices for raw materials, (3) More development assistance, (4) Better access for their manufactured goods in developed countries' markets.",
          "**End of Bretton Woods:** In the 1970s, US financial strength weakened, leading to the collapse of fixed exchange rates and introduction of **floating exchange rates**. Multinational Corporations (MNCs) relocated manufacturing operations to low-wage developing countries like China, India, and Southeast Asia."
        ]
