          "Pre-colonial trade operated through vibrant ports: **Surat** on the Gujarat coast linked India to the Gulf and Red Sea; **Hoogly** in Bengal had links with Southeast Asian ports.",
          "**Decline of Old Ports:** By the 1750s, this network controlled by Indian merchants broke down. The European companies gradually gained power—first securing concessions from local courts, then monopoly rights to trade. Exports from Surat and Hoogly fell dramatically, while **Bombay and Calcutta** grew as colonial ports.",
          "**The East India Company & Weavers:** After establishing political power, the Company eliminated existing traders and brokers to establish direct control over weavers through paid servants called **Gomasthas** (who supervised weavers, collected supplies, and examined cloth quality).",
          "Weavers were prevented from dealing with other buyers through a system of advances/loans: once an advance was accepted, weavers had to hand over the cloth produced to the Gomastha. Clashes were frequent between weavers and arrogant Gomasthas (who marched into villages with sepoys and peons, punishing delays with floggings). Many weavers abandoned looms and migrated to other villages."
