          "**2. Wheat:** Second most important cereal; main food crop in north and north-western India.",
          "**Requirements:** Rabi crop, cool growing season, bright sunshine at ripening, **50 to 75 cm** annual rainfall evenly distributed. Two major wheat zones: Ganga-Satluj plains in north-west and black soil region of Deccan (Punjab, Haryana, UP, MP, Bihar, Rajasthan).",
          "**3. Millets (Coarse Grains):** High nutritional value. (1) **Jowar:** 3rd most important food crop with respect to area and production. Rain-fed crop grown in moist areas (Maharashtra largest producer, Karnataka, AP, MP). (2) **Bajra:** Grows well on sandy soils and shallow black soil (Rajasthan largest, UP, Maharashtra, Gujarat). (3) **Ragi:** Rich in iron, calcium, roughage; grows in dry regions on red, black, sandy, loamy soils (Karnataka largest, Tamil Nadu, Himachal, Uttarakhand).",
          "**4. Maize:** Used both as food and fodder. Kharif crop requiring 21°C–27°C, grows well in old alluvial soil. In Bihar, maize is also grown in rabi season (Karnataka, MP, UP, Bihar, AP).",
          "**5. Pulses:** India is the largest producer and consumer of pulses in the world; major source of plant protein. Tur (arhar), urad, moong, masur, peas, gram. Pulses are leguminous crops that help restore soil fertility by fixing atmospheric nitrogen (except arhar). Grown in rotation with other crops (MP, Rajasthan, Maharashtra, UP, Karnataka)."
