          "**2. Oilseeds:** Cover ~12% of total cropped area. Main oilseeds: Groundnut, mustard, coconut, sesamum (til), soybean, castor seed, cotton seed, linseed, sunflower. Most are edible and used as cooking mediums; some used as raw materials in soap, cosmetics, ointments.",
          "• **Groundnut:** Kharif crop, accounts for half of major oilseeds produced in India (Gujarat largest producer, followed by Rajasthan and Tamil Nadu). Linseed and mustard are rabi crops. Sesamum is kharif in north and rabi in south. Castor seed is grown both as rabi and kharif.",
          "**3. Tea:** Beverage crop introduced initially by the British. Plantation crop requiring warm and moist frost-free climate throughout the year, frequent showers evenly distributed, deep and fertile well-drained soil rich in humus and organic matter. Labour-intensive (Assam, hills of Darjeeling and Jalpaiguri in WB, Tamil Nadu, Kerala).",
          "**4. Coffee:** Indian coffee is renowned for its good quality. The **Arabica** variety initially brought from Yemen is produced in India. Cultivation introduced on the **Baba Budan Hills** and confined to the **Nilgiris** in Karnataka, Kerala, and Tamil Nadu."
