          "**2. Language Policy:** The Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. **Hindi was identified as the official language** (spoken by ~40% of Indians). The Constitution recognizes **22 Scheduled Languages** in the Eighth Schedule. Candidates for Central Government examinations can opt to take the examination in any of these languages. English was continued alongside Hindi for official purposes to prevent anti-Hindi agitations (especially in Tamil Nadu).",
          "**3. Centre–State Relations:** For a long time after independence, the same party ruled at the Centre and in most states; the Centre frequently misused Article 356 to dismiss state governments controlled by rival parties.",
          "**Rise of Coalition Governments (Post-1990):** Regional political parties emerged across states. No single party secured a clear majority in Lok Sabha, leading to the era of **Coalition Governments** at the Centre. Supported by a landmark Supreme Court judgment making it difficult for the Central Government to dismiss state governments arbitrarily, respecting state autonomy."
