        "title": "4. Fundamental Consumer Rights",
        "points": [
          "**(1) Right to Safety:** Consumers have the right to be protected against the marketing of goods and delivery of services that are hazardous to life and property (e.g. defective pressure cooker valves causing explosion).",
          "**(2) Right to be Informed:** Consumers have the right to be informed about the particulars of goods and services: ingredients used, batch number, date of manufacture, expiry date, directions for proper use, and Maximum Retail Price (MRP). In October 2005, the Government of India enacted the **Right to Information (RTI) Act**, ensuring citizens access to information about the functions of government departments.",
          "**(3) Right to Choose:** Any consumer who receives a service in whatever capacity has the right to choose whether to continue that service or not (e.g. a gas agency cannot force you to buy a new stove when taking a gas connection).",
          "**(4) Right to be Heard:** Consumers have the right to present their grievances and receive due consideration in appropriate forums.",
          "**(5) Right to Seek Redressal:** Consumers have the right to seek redressal against unfair trade practices or exploitation. If any damage is done to a consumer, they have the right to get compensation depending on the degree of damage.",
          "**(6) Right to Consumer Education:** Consumers have the right to acquire knowledge and skills to be informed consumers throughout life (promoted through campaigns like **'Jago Grahak Jago'**)."
        ]
